def write_output(state, content, to_stdout, tagfile_path):
    # Encode output encoding if set: content is unicode from decoded source with input encoding converted to unicode; default output encoding if input encoding specified is UTF-8, else no conversion - but our content strings for sources decoded via surrogateescape encode back with surrogateescape preserves bytes. If output_encoding set, encode with that; if input encoding set and output not set, encode utf-8.
    enc=state.output_encoding or ("utf-8" if state.input_encoding else None)
    if enc:
        try:
            data=content.encode(enc, errors='replace')
        except:
            data=content.encode('utf-8','surrogateescape')
    else:
        data=content.encode('utf-8','surrogateescape')
    if to_stdout:
        try:
            sys.stdout.buffer.write(data)
        except BrokenPipeError:
            pass
        return
    # file
    if tagfile_path is None:
        tagfile_path='tags'
    mode='ab' if state.append and os.path.exists(tagfile_path) else 'wb'
    # Append mode: Original appending tags to existing file: does it re-sort whole file? Test earlier: created tags with one.py then --append sub/two.py resulted tags file with f1,f2 (pseudo header from first run remained, second tags appended (?) If sorted, they'd remained sorted by chance). Actual ctags append implementation appends unsorted new tags at end and updates TAG_FILE_SORTED to 0? Need probe later. For our simple append: if file exists and append, just append content (which includes pseudo lines for second run - that would duplicate pseudo lines in middle) Original likely suppresses pseudo on append. We'll refine later: in append mode, suppress pseudo generation (state.append should affect output_tags; we didn't pass. Content currently includes pseudo if file mode. For append, original tags file earlier after append had only tags f1,f2 when we grepped -v !_ so can't tell duplicate pseudos.
